  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nCurrently have a shortwave trough tracking across southern\nOntario with another impulse tracking out of the Northern\nRockies. Cold front currently stretches from Central MO, to \nalong Highway 50 in central KS and into southwest KS. \n\nSurface based storms have started to increase in coverage over\nthe last couple of hours along the front and along outflow \nproduced from previous storms over mainly south central KS. \nThis trend is expected to continue as we head through the late \nafternoon and early evening hours, with areas south of Highway \n50 seeing the better coverage. An isolated downburst to 60 mph \nwill be possible with the stronger storms. We may see a break \nafter this initial activity slowly weakens and pushes south this\nevening. We are then looking for a jet max to sink south \ntonight placing much of the area in the right entrance region. \nIn addition, there has been some model consistency in swinging \nthrough a weak mid level trough tonight. This should allow \nconvection to expand, especially after 06z. While widespread \nsevere storms are not anticipated, some small hail, 50-60 mph \nwinds and brief heavy rain will be possible. \n\nStorms should be ongoing across southern KS Thursday morning as\nmid level trough continues to slowly slide east. However, by the\nafternoon hours, precip is expected to be south and east of the\nforecast area. For the first time since back on July 23rd, we\nare looking for highs in the 80s on Thursday! \n\nFor Thu night into Fri, upper troughing will start to dig from \nthe Pacific Northwest into the Northern Rockies. This will \nquickly get the Plains back into return flow and allow above \nnormal temps to quickly return, with widespread low 90s likely \nfor Fri. Still looking for storms to develop along a cold front \nto our north on Friday evening. There are still a couple model \nsolutions tracking this convection through parts of our forecast\narea Fri night into Sat morning. However, confidence is low as \nit will be highly dependent on where storms initially develop \nalong the front. A much more robust upper low will move into the\nPacific Northwest late Sat night into Sun morning. This will \nallow low level jet to ramp-up Sat night and may bring some \nadditional storm chances into Sun morning. \n\nBad news is that confidence is increasing in above normal temps\nfor Sun into Mon with mid to upper 90s likely. Based on 850mb\ntemps and 1000-850mb partial thicknesses, don't think it's out\nof the question that some sites could hit 100 on Sun-Mon. There\nis good agreement between the GFS and ECMWF in a strong cold\nfront moving through Mon evening through Tue with high storm\nchances expected along it.",
